{"name":"Biscay Green","hex":"#55c6a9","fetched":"2026-10-11","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"green","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The color of grass and leaves; a primary additive color midway between yellow and blue which is evoked by light between roughly 495–570 nm.","A member of a green party; an environmentalist."]},{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Of a green hue.","Sickly, unwell."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/green"}],"entries":[{"title":"Biscay","blocks":[{"text":"The Province of Biscay is a province of the Basque Autonomous Community, heir of the ancient Lordship of Biscay, lying on the south shore of the eponymous bay. The capital and largest city is Bilbao."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a4/Biscay_in_Spain_%28plus_Canarias%29.svg/330px-Biscay_in_Spain_%28plus_Canarias%29.svg.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Biscay","wikidata":"Q93366"},{"title":"Green","blocks":[{"text":"Green is the color between cyan and yellow on the visible spectrum. It has 295–350 different shades. It is evoked by light which has a dominant wavelength of roughly 495–570 nm. In subtractive color systems, used in painting and color printing, it is created by a combination of yellow and cyan; in the RGB color model, used on television and computer screens, it is one of the additive primary colors, along with red and blue, which are mixed in different combinations to create all other colors. By far the largest contributor to green in nature is chlorophyll, the chemical by which plants photosynthesize and convert sunlight into chemical energy. Many creatures have adapted to their green environments by taking on a green hue themselves as camouflage. Several minerals have a green color, including the emerald, which is colored green by its chromium content."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/23/Champ_de_bl%C3%A9_C%C3%B4te-d%27Or_Bourgogne_avril_2014.jpg/330px-Champ_de_bl%C3%A9_C%C3%B4te-d%27Or_Bourgogne_avril_2014.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Green","wikidata":"Q3133"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/08/Santimamine-Santimami%C3%B1e.JPG/500px-Santimamine-Santimami%C3%B1e.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/08/Santimamine-Santimami%C3%B1e.JPG/1280px-Santimamine-Santimami%C3%B1e.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/08/Santimamine-Santimami%C3%B1e.JPG/500px-Santimamine-Santimami%C3%B1e.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Entrance of Santimamiñe cave, in Busturialdea.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ASantimamine-Santimami%C3%B1e.JPG"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/78/Forua%2C_poblado_romano.JPG/500px-Forua%2C_poblado_romano.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/78/Forua%2C_poblado_romano.JPG/1280px-Forua%2C_poblado_romano.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/78/Forua%2C_poblado_romano.JPG/500px-Forua%2C_poblado_romano.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Roman town remains in Forua.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AForua%2C_poblado_romano.JPG"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e2/Portugalete_Casa_Torre_Lope_Garcia_de_Salazar.jpg/500px-Portugalete_Casa_Torre_Lope_Garcia_de_Salazar.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/w